# Clean Code — Naming

## NAME-01 — Choose descriptive and unambiguous names

A name should answer why it exists, what it does, and how it is used. A name that requires a comment to explain it has already failed.

```
// bad
int d  // elapsed time in days

// good
int elapsedDays
```

## NAME-02 — Make meaningful distinctions

Do not create names that differ only in arbitrary noise. If two things are different, their names must express the difference.

```
// bad — what is the distinction between these two?
function getActiveAccount()
function getActiveAccountInfo()

// bad — number suffixes communicate nothing
copyChars(a1, a2)

// good — names express the actual roles
copyChars(source, destination)
```

## NAME-03 — Use pronounceable names

If you cannot say the name aloud in a conversation, it will slow down code reviews and discussion.

```
// bad
genymdhms   // generation year/month/day/hour/minute/second

// good
generationTimestamp
```

## NAME-04 — Use searchable names

Avoid single-letter names and bare numeric literals for anything other than short loop counters. A name must be findable with a text search.

```
// bad
for i = 0 to 5
  sum += values[i] * 4

// good
WORK_DAYS_PER_WEEK = 5
MULTIPLIER = 4

for day = 0 to WORK_DAYS_PER_WEEK
  sum += values[day] * MULTIPLIER
```

## NAME-05 — Replace magic numbers with named constants

A bare literal in logic is opaque to every reader including the original author six months later.

```
// bad
if age >= 18

// good
LEGAL_ADULT_AGE = 18
if age >= LEGAL_ADULT_AGE
```

## NAME-06 — Avoid encodings and type prefixes

Do not append type information or Hungarian-notation prefixes. Types are visible in modern tooling; encoding them into names adds noise without information.

```
// bad
strUserName = "Alice"
iRetryCount = 3
class IPaymentGateway

// good
userName = "Alice"
retryCount = 3
class PaymentGateway
```
